(1) A credit institution that is registered in Latvia, or is a branch in Latvia of a foreign credit institution or of a credit institution registered in another Member State, and that provides financial services in Latvia to consumers resident in the European Union (hereinafter in this Section 'the consumer'), must, when providing financial services to its customers who are consumers, comply with the following conditions:
1) provide the customer with convenient means of applying for financial services in at least two different ways, in compliance with the accessibility requirements for services laid down in laws and regulations;
2) take into account that not every customer may have access to electronic identification means and means of distance communication, or sufficient skills and ability to use them;
3) in accordance with criteria set by the credit institution, identify groups of customers who may find it difficult, without additional support, to receive financial services remotely by means of distance communication, inform those customers of the support provided by the credit institution and of the availability of financial services in person, and, at its discretion, survey those customers on their habits in using financial services;
4) where a charge is provided for financial services in person, set that charge in proportion to the actual costs of providing financial services in person and at the same level throughout the territory of Latvia, irrespective of the place where the financial services are provided in person;
5) when setting the charge for financial services provided remotely that are equivalent to financial services provided in person, take into account that a difference in the charges for the two ways of providing the services may be justified only by a difference in the actual costs of those ways of providing the services.

(2) At the request of Latvijas Banka, a credit institution referred to in Paragraph 1 of this Section must provide it with information on the justification and calculation of the charges for financial services provided in person and for equivalent financial services provided remotely.

(3) In addition to the cash withdrawal service it provides in accordance with Paragraphs 2, 3 and 4 of Section 73.¹ of this Law, a credit institution referred to in Section 73.¹, Paragraph 1 of this Law must provide its customers who are consumers, in person, with at least the following activities and services necessary for receiving financial services:
1) advice on lending, investment services and savings matters;
2) advice to promote financial literacy;
3) account opening, closing and other account servicing services, including the issuing, renewal or cancellation of any payment instrument;
4) identification of the customer in person and provision of authentication tools, and other actions involving them;
5) the documentation of the legal relationship between the customer and the credit institution, and obtaining the information necessary for entering into, amending or terminating that relationship;
6) carrying out a transfer of money from the customer's payment account to another payment account, by assisting the customer or by completing and executing, on the customer's instructions, an order to transfer money to a payment account.

(4) A credit institution referred to in Section 73.¹, Paragraph 1 of this Law must ensure that the activities and services referred to in Paragraph 3 of this Section are provided to its customers who are consumers in person in accordance with the following requirements:
1) on a permanent basis in each state city that at the same time meets the following conditions:
a) it is located at a distance from the nearest other state city or administrative centre of a municipal territory (hereinafter 'the municipality') in which the credit institution concerned ensures the permanent provision in person of the activities and services referred to in Paragraph 3 of this Section that is too great to ensure reasonable access to financial services in person for the inhabitants of the municipality concerned,
b) the number of the credit institution's customers who are natural persons and who have stated to the credit institution the administrative territory of the local government concerned as their place of residence makes up a significant proportion of the total number of inhabitants of that administrative territory;
2) on a permanent basis in each administrative centre of a municipality that is not a state city and that at the same time meets the following conditions:
a) the administrative centre of the municipality is located at a distance from the nearest other administrative centre of a municipality or state city in which the credit institution concerned ensures the permanent provision in person of the activities and services referred to in Paragraph 3 of this Section that is too great to ensure reasonable access to financial services in person for the inhabitants of the municipality concerned,
b) the number of inhabitants of the municipality, also taking into account how it is changing, is large enough to ensure sufficient demand for financial services in person,
c) the number of the credit institution's customers who are natural persons and who have stated to the credit institution the municipality concerned as their place of residence makes up a significant proportion of the total number of inhabitants of the municipality;
3) in each state city and administrative centre of a municipality in which the credit institution is not required to ensure the permanent provision in person of the activities and services referred to in Paragraph 3 of this Section, at the customer's request, in accordance with the solution for providing financial services in person chosen by the credit institution;
4) in the state cities and administrative centres of municipalities referred to in Clauses 1 and 2 of this Paragraph, the permanent availability in person of the activities and services referred to in Paragraph 3 of this Section must be ensured in accordance with demand, but for no less than the minimum opening hours of service points set by Latvijas Banka;
5) where the activities and services referred to in Paragraph 3 of this Section are provided to the customer in person after a prior application has been received, they must be provided no later than within the time limit set by Latvijas Banka.

(5) Latvijas Banka may determine that a credit institution referred to in Section 73.¹, Paragraph 1 of this Law must provide some of the activities and services referred to in Paragraph 3 of this Section in person on a permanent basis in a state city or administrative centre of a municipality referred to in Clause 3 of Paragraph 4 of this Section.

(6) Latvijas Banka may allow a credit institution referred to in Section 73.¹, Paragraph 1 of this Law not to comply with the requirements laid down in Clause 3 of Paragraph 4 of this Section in particular state cities or administrative centres of municipalities where all the following conditions are met:
1) the number of the credit institution's customers who are natural persons and who have stated to the credit institution the administrative territory of the local government concerned as their place of residence does not make up a significant proportion of the total number of inhabitants of that administrative territory;
2) the state city or administrative centre of the municipality is located at a distance from the nearest other state city or administrative centre of a municipality in which the credit institution concerned ensures the permanent provision in person of the activities and services referred to in Paragraph 3 of this Section that is sufficient to ensure reasonable access to financial services in person for the inhabitants of the administrative territory of the local government concerned;
3) the credit institution demonstrates that the availability of financial services will be ensured for its customers who are consumers in the administrative territory of the local government concerned.

(7) Where a credit institution referred to in Section 73.¹, Paragraph 1 of this Law decides to close a permanent place for providing financial services in person whose closure does not affect the compliance of the credit institution's activities with the requirements referred to in Clauses 1 and 2 of Paragraph 4 of this Section, or to reduce significantly the range of financial services available in person at such a permanent place, the credit institution must:
1) before implementing the decision, prepare a justification for it, also assessing the effect of the decision on customers and the financial services available to them;
2) inform the customers, Latvijas Banka and the local government in whose administrative territory the permanent place for providing financial services in person is located as early as possible, and no later than 30 days in advance;
3) provide customers with all the necessary information and advisory support to ensure that they are properly informed of where, how and in accordance with what procedure they will be able to receive the financial services and practical support they need after the permanent place for providing financial services in person has been closed.

(8) Latvijas Banka may release a credit institution in whole or in part from the obligations laid down in this Section where, in the view of Latvijas Banka, this does not significantly affect the availability of financial services as a whole, including having regard to the proportion of the credit institution's customers who are consumers with sufficient skills in using electronic identification means and means of distance communication, and does not significantly reduce the financial inclusion and economic activity of particular groups of the population in the administrative territories of local governments or in the country as a whole. When deciding to release a credit institution from the obligations laid down in this Section, Latvijas Banka may, on grounds of expediency, impose on the credit institution obligations replacing the performance of the obligations concerned.

(9) Latvijas Banka lays down:
1) the conditions referred to in Clauses 1 and 2 of Paragraph 4 and in Paragraph 6 of this Section on the distance within which access to financial services in person must be ensured, on the significant proportion of the credit institution's customers who are natural persons in the administrative territory of a local government and on the number of inhabitants of a municipality sufficient to ensure demand for financial services in person, and the methodology and threshold values for applying those conditions;
2) the requirements referred to in Clauses 4 and 5 of Paragraph 4 of this Section on the opening hours of the places for providing in person the activities and services referred to in Paragraph 3 of this Section and on the time limit within which those activities and services are provided to the customer in person after a prior application has been received, and the conditions for applying those requirements;
3) the range of activities and services to be provided in person on a permanent basis in a state city or administrative centre of a municipality referred to in Clause 3 of Paragraph 4 of this Section, the opening hours of the places where they are provided and the time limit for providing those activities and services, for the purposes of applying Paragraph 5 of this Section;
4) the requirements, time limit and procedure by which a credit institution referred to in Section 73.¹, Paragraph 1 of this Law prepares and submits to Latvijas Banka information on compliance with the requirements referred to in this Section.

(10) Once every two years, Latvijas Banka reports to the Saeimas Budžeta un finanšu (nodokļu) komisija on the performance of the obligations of credit institutions laid down in this Section. Latvijas Banka provides an assessment of the effectiveness of the legal framework laid down in this Section and of the expediency of continuing to apply it, taking into account changes in the demand for financial services in person and other ways of providing financial services offered on the market, as well as an assessment of the availability of financial services and of the financial inclusion of all groups of society.

(In the wording of the Law of 19.09.2024, which enters into force on 09.10.2024. The Section enters into force on 01.01.2026. See Clause 122 of the Transitional Provisions)

