(1) A management company must ensure an internal control system that minimises the possibility of conflicts of interest arising between:
1) the management company and its clients;
2) the funds under the management company's management;
3) the management company's clients;
4) the management company's clients and the funds under the management company's management;
5) an alternative investment fund or its investors and an investment fund under the management company's management or the investors in that investment fund;
6) the management company and a joint-stock company in whose shares the management company invests the assets of an investment fund, of the state funded pension scheme or of pension plans established by private pension funds, where the registered address of that joint-stock company is in a Member State and its shares are admitted to a regulated market of a Member State.

(2) In order to identify the types of conflicts of interest that may arise in the course of providing management services and carrying out activities that may damage the interests of a fund, the management company must take into account situations in which the management company, a person connected with the management company or a person who (directly or indirectly) controls the management company:
1) is likely to make a financial gain, or avoid a financial loss, at the expense of the fund;
2) has an interest in the outcome of a service provided to the fund or another client, or of a transaction carried out on behalf of the fund, which is distinct from the fund's interest;
3) has a financial or other incentive to favour the interest of another client or group of clients over the interests of the fund;
4) carries on the same activities for the fund and for a client or group of clients that is not a fund;
5) receives or will receive from a person other than the fund, in relation to the management service provided to the fund, an inducement in the form of money, goods or services other than the standard fee for that service.

(3) When identifying the types of conflicts of interest, the management company must take into account:
1) the interests of the management company, including those deriving from its belonging to a group or from the performance of services and activities, as well as the interests of clients and the management company's duty towards the fund;
2) the interests of two or more funds under management.

(3¹) When identifying the types of conflicts of interest whose existence may damage the interests of a fund, the management company must also take into account the types of conflicts of interest that may arise from the integration of sustainability risks in its processes and systems, including its internal control system.

(4) In order to ensure compliance with the requirements of Paragraph 1 of this Section, the management company must draw up a conflicts of interest policy appropriate to its size and organisation and to the specific nature, scale and complexity of its transactions. Where the management company is a member of a group of companies, the conflicts of interest policy must also provide for the prevention of conflicts of interest that may arise from the activities or structure of another company in the group.

(5) In its conflicts of interest policy, the management company must:
1) identify the circumstances that constitute or may give rise to a conflict of interest entailing a material risk of damage or harm to the interests of the fund or of one or more clients in connection with the management services provided by the management company or by a third party on its behalf;
2) lay down the procedures and measures necessary to prevent conflicts of interest.

(6) In laying down the procedures and measures for preventing conflicts of interest, the management company must ensure that they are proportionate to the size and professional activities of the management company itself or of the group of which it is a member, and to the materiality of the risk of damage to the interests of clients.

(7) In meeting the requirements of Clause 2 of Paragraph 5 of this Section, the management company must, in accordance with its structure and the types of services it provides, provide for:
1) effective procedures to prevent or control the exchange of information between persons connected with the management company who are involved in the provision of management services and whose activities involve a risk of a conflict of interest, where that exchange of information may harm the interests of one or more clients;
2) the separate supervision of persons connected with the management company whose principal functions involve providing management services on behalf of clients, or providing services to clients or investors in a fund whose interests may conflict or who otherwise represent different interests that may conflict, including with the interests of the management company;
3) the removal of any direct link between the remuneration or income of persons connected with the management company who are engaged in providing different management services, where a conflict of interest may arise in relation to the activities carried out in the process of providing management services;
4) measures to prevent or limit any third party from exercising inappropriate influence over the management services carried out by a person connected with the management company;
5) measures to prevent or control the simultaneous or sequential involvement of persons connected with the management company in different activities forming part of the process of providing management services, where such involvement may impair the proper management of conflicts of interest;
6) other additional procedures and measures necessary and appropriate to prevent a conflict of interest from arising, where the organisational or administrative arrangements made by the management company in accordance with the requirements of this Section to manage conflicts of interest are not sufficient.

(8) The management company must keep, and regularly update, information on the investment fund management activities carried out by it or on its behalf that have given rise to, or, where those activities are ongoing, may give rise to, a conflict of interest entailing a material risk of damage to the interests of one or more funds or other clients.

(9) Persons involved in the provision of management services must inform the management board of the management company of any case where the organisational or administrative arrangements made by the management company in accordance with the requirements of this Section to manage conflicts of interest are not sufficient to ensure, with reasonable confidence, that risks of damage to the interests of the fund or its unit-holders will be prevented. Once the management board of the management company has been informed of a case referred to in this Paragraph, it must take the decisions necessary to ensure that the interests of the fund and its unit-holders are protected.

(10) The management company must inform investors, by means of any appropriate durable medium, of the cases referred to in Paragraph 9 of this Section and of the decision taken.

(11) The management company must ensure that one and the same employee of the management company performs only one of the following functions:
1) managing the financial instruments owned by the management company and carrying out, or transmitting for execution, the related tasks;
2) individually managing the fund's investments and clients' financial instruments and carrying out, or transmitting for execution, the related tasks;
3) recording transactions in financial instruments.

(12) The management company must draw up a procedure for exercising the voting rights attached to the financial instruments held in the fund's investment portfolio. That procedure must set out the measures necessary to:
1) monitor and ensure that voting rights are exercised in accordance with the investment objectives and policy of the fund concerned;
2) prevent or manage any conflicts of interest arising from the exercise of voting rights.

(13) The management company must publish on its website a summary description of the procedure referred to in Paragraph 12 of this Section and must, on request and free of charge, inform the fund's investors of the actions taken on the basis of that procedure.

(14) The management company has no right to invest its own funds in another management company, nor to acquire units of a fund it manages itself.

(15) The restrictions laid down in Paragraph 14 of this Section on the acquisition by the management company of units of a fund it manages itself do not apply where the management company takes over the management of a fund established by another management company. In that case, within six months from the day on which the takeover is completed, the management company must take appropriate measures to bring its further activities into line with Paragraph 14 of this Section.

(16) Where a management company manages clients' individual portfolios of financial instruments, including the assets of pension plans established by a private pension fund, it must not invest the assets in such a portfolio in funds under its management, unless such authority has been expressly granted in the contract concluded with the client for the provision of that service.

(In the wording of the Law of 13.10.2011, as amended by the Laws of 09.07.2013, 20.06.2019 and 28.04.2022, which enter into force on 31.05.2022. Paragraph 3.¹ enters into force on 01.08.2022. See Clause 46 of the Transitional Provisions)

