(2) A consumer credit agreement must be drawn up in writing (on paper or on another durable medium), and one copy of the agreement must be given to each contracting party. Payment for goods, a service, digital content or a digital service must be stated in money.

(2¹) It is prohibited to conclude or amend a consumer credit agreement between 21.00 and 8.00, unless the laws and regulations on consumer lending provide for stricter restrictions on the provision of consumer lending services.

(2²) The costs of a consumer credit agreement must be proportionate and consistent with fair trading practice. The total cost of the credit to the consumer is calculated in accordance with the procedure laid down in the laws and regulations on consumer lending.

(2³) A total cost of the credit to the consumer that exceeds 0.07 per cent of the amount of credit per day is regarded as not meeting the requirements referred to in Paragraph 2.² of this Section. The limit on the total cost of the credit to the consumer does not apply to consumer credit agreements on the conclusion of which an item must be placed in the creditor's custody as security and under which the consumer's liability is limited to the pledged item alone.

(2⁴) In a consumer credit agreement under which the consumer must repay the credit within a period not exceeding three months, the total of all costs related to the credit, including interest, default interest, contractual penalties, commissions, deferrals of payment and other payments related to the performance of the credit agreement, must not exceed the amount of the credit granted. This rule does not apply to:
1) credit agreements on the conclusion of which an item must be placed in the creditor's custody as security and under which the consumer's liability is limited to the pledged item alone;
2) agreements on an overdraft facility;
3) agreements on the conclusion of which the creditor has tacitly accepted that the consumer uses funds exceeding the current balance in the consumer's payment account or the overdraft facility granted.

(2⁵) Where a consumer credit agreement has been concluded by means of distance communication, the credit must be repaid in instalments proportionate to the term of the agreement and to the amount of interest and principal, at least once a month, except for consumer credit agreements:
1) whose repayment term does not exceed one month;
2) on the conclusion of which the creditor grants the consumer an overdraft facility or a credit to be drawn using a payment card issued by the creditor;
3) under which the creditor must defer, in whole or in part, the performance of credit obligations of a soldier of the state defence service performing 11 months of state defence military service, or of a person performing 11 months of state defence civil service as alternative service.

(2⁶) A term of a consumer credit agreement that allows default interest to be claimed from the consumer is void where that interest exceeds the borrowing rate by more than 36 percentage points per year. This condition does not apply to a consumer credit agreement under which repayment of the credit is secured by a mortgage on immovable property, or which has been concluded by a person referred to in Clause 10 of Paragraph 4.³ of this Section.

(2⁷) It is prohibited to stipulate bills of exchange as a means of payment in consumer lending.

(2⁸) In a consumer credit agreement concluded for a period of up to 30 days that provides for repayment of the credit in a single payment:
1) the amount of the loan must not exceed 50 per cent of the national minimum monthly wage. This rule does not apply to credit agreements on the conclusion of which an item must be placed in the creditor's custody as security and under which the consumer's liability is limited to the pledged item alone;
2) the repayment term of the credit must not be extended more than twice. This rule does not apply where a repayment schedule is offered under which the principal of the credit is to be repaid in instalments.

(3) A consumer has the right at any time to discharge his or her obligations, in full or in part, before the term laid down in the consumer credit agreement. In that case the consumer is entitled to a fair reduction in the total cost of the credit, consisting of the interest and other costs for the remaining term of the agreement. That right of the consumer does not apply to the credit agreements referred to in Clauses 2 and 4 of Section 12.¹, Paragraph 8 of this Law.

(3¹) For late payment or for the recovery of a debt out of court, a creditor may charge the consumer only the creditor's actual costs, which must be proportionate and objectively justified.

(3²) (Paragraph enters into force on 20.11.2026 and is included in the wording of the Law as of 20.11.2026. See Clause 47 of the Transitional Provisions)

(4) Ministru kabinets lays down:
1) the content of the information to be provided before a consumer credit agreement is concluded and the procedure for providing it;
2) the conditions for providing ancillary services;
3) the requirements for a consumer credit agreement and the information it must contain;
4) the method of calculating the annual percentage rate of charge;
5) the conditions for credits in a foreign currency and at a variable interest rate;
6) how consumers are to be informed during the term of a credit agreement;
7) early repayment of credit and the fair reduction of the total cost of the credit;
8) the requirements applicable to particular types of credit agreements;
9) the requirements for providing advice;
10) the obligations of credit intermediaries and appointed representatives of credit intermediaries;
11) the legal framework for consumer lending against a pledge of movable property;
12) the procedure and time limits for carrying out mortgage refinancing;
13) the obligations of a creditor where a soldier of the state defence service performing 11 months of state defence military service, or a person performing 11 months of state defence civil service as alternative service, has credit obligations whose performance must be deferred in whole or in part;
14) (Clause enters into force on 01.07.2027 and is included in the wording of the Law as of 01.07.2027. See Clause 56 of the Transitional Provisions).

(4¹) Before a consumer credit agreement is concluded, the creditor must assess the consumer's ability to repay the credit on the basis of sufficient information obtained from the consumer and, where necessary, of information on the person's income and performance of payment obligations from databases set up for processing personal data in accordance with laws and regulations (hereinafter 'the database'), obtained in accordance with the procedure laid down in the laws and regulations on the protection of personal data of natural persons and on access to databases. Where a refusal to grant credit is based on information obtained from a database, the creditor must inform the consumer immediately and free of charge of the result of the database consultation and provide details of the database consulted.

(4²) Before the consumer and the creditor agree to increase significantly the total amount of credit during the term of the consumer credit agreement, the creditor must update the financial information it holds on the consumer and reassess the consumer's ability to repay the credit. Where a refusal to increase the total amount of credit is based on information obtained from a database, the creditor must inform the consumer immediately and free of charge of the result of the database consultation and provide details of the database consulted.

(4³) The obligations laid down in Paragraphs 4.¹, 4.², 4.⁴ and 4.⁷ of this Section do not apply to consumer credit agreements:
1) (deleted as of 01.07.2014 by the Law of 24.04.2014. See Clause 21 of the Transitional Provisions);
2) under which the creditor grants the consumer the right to use funds exceeding the current balance in the consumer's payment account (hereinafter 'the overdraft facility') and which must be repaid within a period not exceeding one month;
3) under which the creditor has tacitly accepted that the consumer uses funds exceeding the current balance in the consumer's payment account or the overdraft facility granted;
4) on the conclusion of which an item must be placed in the creditor's custody as security and under which the consumer's liability is limited to the pledged item alone;
5) concluded between an employer and an employee, where the credit is granted free of interest or at an annual percentage rate of charge lower than the interest rates prevailing on the market and not generally offered to the public, and where granting credit is not the employer's main business;
6) concluded with investment firms or credit institutions to enable an investor to carry out a transaction in one or more instruments regulated under the laws and regulations on the financial instruments market, where the investment firm or credit institution granting the credit is involved in that transaction;
7) which are the result of a settlement reached in court or before another body designated by laws and regulations;
8) which relate to the discharge of a debt by deferred payments, free of interest and other additional charges;
9) for loans granted in the general interest, in accordance with the procedure laid down in laws and regulations, to a limited group of persons at interest rates lower than those prevailing on the market, or free of interest, or on other terms more favourable to the consumer than those prevailing on the market, and at interest rates not higher than those prevailing on the market;
10) concluded by a manufacturer, seller or service provider for the purchase of goods or services in the form of a deferred payment, a loan or another similar financial accommodation, under which the credit is granted free of interest and other additional charges;
11) under which the consumer must repay the credit within a period not exceeding three months and only insignificant additional charges are required for the use of the credit compared with the total amount of credit and the term of the credit agreement;
12) concluded in the course of mortgage refinancing, where the interest rate stays the same or decreases and the other terms of the original credit agreement are not changed in a way that would increase the total cost of the credit to the consumer;
13) (Clause enters into force on 20.11.2026 and is included in the wording of the Law as of 20.11.2026. See Clause 47 of the Transitional Provisions);
14) (Clause enters into force on 20.11.2026 and is included in the wording of the Law as of 20.11.2026. See Clause 47 of the Transitional Provisions).

(4⁴) In fulfilling the obligation laid down in Paragraphs 4.¹ and 4.² of this Section, the creditor must request, obtain and assess information on the consumer's income and expenditure to the extent appropriate for fulfilling that obligation. The consumer must provide information on his or her income and expenditure at the creditor's request. The creditor may rely on information obtained solely from the consumer, where it is sufficient and supported by documents. The creditor may grant credit only after it has assessed the consumer's ability to repay the credit and the assessment indicates that the credit obligations are likely to be met in accordance with the terms of the agreement. Where a credit application is rejected, the creditor must inform the consumer of the rejection without delay and, where applicable, that the decision-making process is automated.

(4⁵) Where a creditor grants credit to a consumer without assessing the consumer's ability to repay it, the creditor may not require the consumer to pay more than the statutory interest for the use of the money granted under the credit agreement, nor apply to the consumer any means of reinforcing obligations or any compensation. Where such a credit agreement was concluded when the consumer had not reached the age of 20, the creditor may not claim payment of statutory interest either. This Paragraph applies only to means of reinforcing obligations or compensation connected with late payment, where the late payment results from the consumer's ability to repay the credit not having been assessed in accordance with the requirements of this Section when the credit was granted. This Paragraph does not release the consumer from the obligation to repay the outstanding total amount of credit in accordance with the credit agreement.

(4⁶) In actions for recovery of money brought by a creditor, the consumer has the right to ask the court to reduce the interest payable under the credit agreement in accordance with Paragraph 4.⁵ of this Section. In the event of a dispute, the creditor is deemed not to have fulfilled the obligation laid down in Paragraphs 4.¹, 4.² and 4.⁴ of this Section to assess the consumer's ability to repay the credit, unless the creditor proves otherwise. The right to claim a reduction of the interest payable under a consumer credit agreement in accordance with Paragraph 4.⁵ of this Section becomes time-barred five years after the date on which the consumer credit agreement was concluded or the total amount of credit was significantly increased.

(4⁷) So that creditors can, in accordance with the requirements of this Law, assess a consumer's ability to repay the credit where the information the consumer has provided is not sufficient and supported by documents, and a guarantor's ability to meet the obligations arising from a contract of guarantee, creditors must exchange with one another, through credit information bureaus, information on the consumer or the guarantor, their obligations and the performance of those obligations to the extent sufficient for carrying out the assessment.

(4⁸) So that, in fulfilling the obligations laid down in Paragraphs 4.¹, 4.² and 4.⁴ of this Section, a creditor has access to the information included in the databases of all credit information bureaus, the credit information bureau with which the creditor has concluded a contract must, at the creditor's request and on its behalf, request and receive from the other credit information bureaus the information held in their databases on the consumer or the guarantor, their obligations and the performance of those obligations.

(4⁹) (Paragraph enters into force on 20.11.2026 and is included in the wording of the Law as of 20.11.2026. See Clause 47 of the Transitional Provisions)

